<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-connected-top-spaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Connected topological spaces</title>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-def-separatedness">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>X</m> is a nonempty topological space. Following statements are equivalent.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    There exists nonempty open subsets <m>U,V</m> of <m>X</m> such that <m>U\cap V=\emptyset</m> and <m>X=U\cup V</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    There exists nonempty closed subsets <m>A,B</m> of <m>X</m> such that <m>A\cap B=\emptyset</m> and <m>X=A\cup B</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    There are nonempty subsets <m>C,D</m> of <m>X</m> such that <m>X=C\cup D</m>. Furthermore, limit points of <m>C</m> are not contained in <m>D</m> and limit points of <m>D</m> are not contained in <m>C</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  <proof>
    <p>
        (1)<m>\implies</m> (2). The complement of <m>U</m> (resp., the complement of <m>V</m>) is the open subset <m>V</m> (resp., <m>U</m>). Therefore, <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> are both open and closed subsets. Hence, we can take <m>A=U</m> and <m>B=V</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
       (2)<m>\implies</m> (1). The complement of <m>A</m> (resp., the complement of <m>B</m>) is the closed subset <m>B</m> (resp., <m>A</m>). Therefore, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are both open and closed subsets. Hence, we can take <m>U=A</m> and <m>V=B</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        (3)<m>\implies</m>(2). Note that <m>\overline{C}=C\cup C'</m> and <m>\overline{D}=D\cup D'</m>, where <m>C'</m> and <m>D'</m> are collections of limit points of <m>C</m> and <m>D</m>, respectively. By the hypothesis, <m>C'\not\subset D</m> and <m>D'\not\subset C</m>; therefore, <m>C'\subseteq C</m> and <m>D'\subseteq D</m>, i.e., <m>C,D</m> are closed subsets and <m>C\cap D=\emptyset</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        (2)<m>\implies</m> (3). We can take <m>C=A</m> and <m>D=B</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>
  <definition xml:id="def-separatedness">
    <statement>
        <p>
            A topological space <m>X</m> is said to be <term>separated</term> if any one (hence all) the conditions of <xref ref="thm-def-separatedness"/> holds.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <definition xml:id="def-connected">
    <statement>
        <p>
            A topological space <m>X</m> is said to be <term>connected</term> if it is <em>not separated</em>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-equivalent-connectedness">
    <statement>
        <p>
            For a topological space <m>X</m> following statements are equivalent.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>X</m> is connected
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If <m>Y\subseteq X</m> is both open and closed then, either <m>Y=\emptyset</m> or <m>Y=X</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If <m>X=A\cup B</m> where, <m>\overline{A}\cap B=\emptyset</m> and <m>\overline{B}\cap A=\emptyset</m> then, either <m>A=\emptyset</m> or <m>B=\emptyset</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Every continuous function <m>f\colon X\to\{0,1\}</m> is constant, where <m>\{0,1\}</m> is given the discrete topolgy.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  <proof>
    <p>
        First three conditions are equivalent follows from the proof of <xref ref="thm-def-separatedness"/>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Suppose that <m>X=U\cup V</m>, where <m>U,V</m> are nonempty disjoint open subsets of <m>X</m>. We define <m>f\colon X\to\{0,1\}</m> such that <m>f(u)=0</m> for every <m>u\in U</m> and <m>f(v)=1</m> for every <m>v\in V</m>. This is a continuous function. This shows that (4)<m>\implies</m> (1).
    </p>
    <p>
        Suppose that <m>X</m> is connected. If there is a continuous function <m>f\colon X\to\{0,1\}</m> which is not constant, then <m>U=f^{-1}\{0\}</m> and <m>V=f^{-1}\{1\}</m> are nonempty disjoint open subsets of <m>X</m> and <m>X=U\cup V</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>
  <example>
    <title>(Connected subset of real line if and only if interval)</title> 
    <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>I</m> is an interval in <m>\R</m>. Suppose that <m>f\colon I\to\{0,1\}</m> be a continuous function. Further assume that <m>a,b\in I</m>, <m>a&lt;b</m>, and that <m>f(a)=0</m>. Consider <m>u=\sup\{c:a\leq c\leq b\text{ and }f(c)=0\}</m>. Note that <m>f(u)=0</m>. Indeed, if <m>f(u)\neq 0</m>, i.e., <m>f(u)=1</m> then, <m>u</m> is a limit point of <m>A=\{c:a\leq c\leq b\text{ and }f(c)=0\}</m>, i.e., <m>u\in\overline{A}</m>. By the continuity of <m>f</m>, we must have <m>f\left(\overline{A}\right)\subseteq\overline{f(A)}</m>. However, <m>\overline{f(A)}=\{0\}</m> while <m>f\left(\overline{A}\right)=\{0,1\}</m> as <m>1=f(u)\in f\left(\overline{A}\right)</m>.

            We claim that <m>u=y</m>. Indeed, if <m>u&lt;y</m> then, <m>u\in\overline{\left([x,y]\setminus A\right)}</m>. Arguing on the similar lines as in the above paragraph we get a contradiction.
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>Y</m> is a connected subset of <m>\R</m>. If <m>a,b\in Y</m> are such that <m>a&lt;b</m> and if <m>y\in\R</m> is such that <m>a&lt;y&lt;b</m> but <m>y\not\in Y</m>. Then, <m>X=\left(X\cap (-\infty,x)\right)\cup\left(X\cap (x,\infty)\right)</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  <example>
    <title>(Product of two connected spaces is connected)</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>X,Y</m> are connected topological spaces. We show that <m>X\times Y</m> is connected.
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>(a,b)\in X\times Y</m> and <m>(x,y)\in X\times Y</m> are distinct points. Consider a continuous function <m>f\colon X\times Y\to \{0,1\}</m>. The subspace <m>\{a\}\times Y\simeq Y</m>, and hence <m>\{a\}\times Y</m> is connected. Therefore, <m>f(a,b)=f(a,y')</m> for every <m>y'\in Y</m>. In particular, <m>f(a,b)=f(a,y)</m>. Similarly, <m>X\times \{y\}</m> is connected and hence, <m>f(x',y)=f(x,y)</m> for every <m>x'\in X</m>. In particular, <m>f(a,y)=f(x,y)</m>. Therefore, we obtain that <m>f(a,b)=f(a,y)=f(x,y)</m>, i.e., <m>f</m> is a constant function.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  <example>
    <title>(Interior of a connected space need not be connected)</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Choose two closed unit discs in <m>\R^2</m> touching at a single point, say <m>D_1</m> centered at <m>(0,0)</m>, and <m>D_2</m> centered at <m>(2,0)</m>. The interior of <m>D_1\cup D_2</m> is the union of two disjoint open unit discs, viz., one centered at <m>(0,0)</m> and the other centered at <m>(2,0)</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  <example>
    <title>(Closure of a connected space is connected)</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Left as an exercise.
        </p>
    </statement>
    
  </example>
</section>